# Proposal: a grid cell

You are shown the documented rules of the recovery check, not its code, and ONE cell of a grid of reply shapes under 'The cell to cover'. The cell is written in a notation: fence[ ... ] is a code fence, a line of three backticks before and after, holding one part per line in the order given; S is one plain sentence such as 'Here is the result:'; A, B and C are small JSON objects different from each other, such as {"a": 1}, {"b": 2}, {"c": 3}; anything after the closing ] comes after the closing fence line, one part per line. Build the input exactly from the cell, parts on their own lines, nothing added. The rewritten text is the same reply with ONE part removed, and say what the rule says the recovered object ought to do: move or stay. Your expectation comes from the rule's words alone: name in the body which object the rule says is scored before and after. The commitments carry "cell": the cell exactly as named. The commitments are a STRING whose content is JSON of the form {"kernel": "<id>", "input": "<the input text>", "rewritten": "<your rewritten text>", "expect": "moves" or "unchanged", "rewrite": "<one sentence naming what you changed>"}. The input and the rewritten text are instances written out in full, never descriptions. Do not repeat a pair shown among the earlier proposals. The commitments also carry "cell": "<the cell, in words>".

## The problem (problem)
The checks under test are the conformance harness's own functions. Most seats are shown only their documented rules, not their code: how the harness recovers a JSON object from a reply, whether it had to recover it from prose, its response verdict, its refusal-phrase check, and its grounding matcher. The question: is there an input and a rewrite of it on which the rule, read as written, says one thing and the check, run by machine, does another? The executor runs the check on both texts and records what its answer did; nothing here decides anything, the record shows where a reading and the machine parted.

## The documented rules of the checks (rules)
[f8b7dbf88099a402] (mini.kernel-rules.v1)
REFUSAL_PHRASES = ['I cannot', "I can't", "I'm sorry", 'I am unable', 'as an AI', 'cannot assist']

The kernels:

- conformance.kernel.recovery: The object the harness recovers from the reply, as canonical JSON, or NO_OBJECT.

- conformance.kernel.recovered-from-prose: Whether the harness had to recover the object from prose or a fence (yes) or read it as strict JSON (no).

- conformance.kernel.response-verdict: The harness's response verdict for the reply: JSON_OBJECT, INVALID_JSON, REFUSAL_SUSPECTED, or another of its verdicts.

- conformance.kernel.refusal-phrase: The first listed refusal phrase the reply contains, typographic apostrophes read as straight, or NONE.

- conformance.kernel.span-occurs: Whether a cited span occurs in a document, whitespace-normalised, no relaxation: the input is one JSON object {"span", "document"}; verbatim or NOT_IN_DOCUMENT.

- conformance.kernel.grounding: The harness's grounding verdict for a value, its cited span and the document, no relaxation, value-in-span checked: the input is one JSON object {"value", "span", "document"}; GROUNDED, SPAN_MISSING, SPAN_NOT_IN_DOCUMENT or VALUE_NOT_IN_SPAN.

def recover_json_object(content: 'str') -> 'Any':
    Project import: extract a JSON object from fences or surrounding prose.

    Every top-level balanced object in the text is a candidate, not only the span from the
    first ``{`` to the last ``}``: reasoning prose before or after the answer frequently
    contains stray braces, and an object nested inside another is not a candidate of its own.
    The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the
    last top-level object in the text, because a model places its final answer last and marks
    it: a reply that quotes its previous answer and then gives a corrected one is scored on the
    correction. Until 9 September 2026 the object with the most keys was taken, ties to the
    last, and nine cycle replies that dropped a criticised key were scored on the draft that
    still carried it (``docs/failure-modes.md``, H40). A candidate that parses as strict JSON,
    or as JSON with repeated keys resolved last-wins, is scoreable; one that does not (a float,
    for instance) is passed over, so a final answer refused for a float loses to an earlier
    draft that parsed (``docs/kernel.md``, P-06).

def _loads_last_wins(candidate: 'str') -> 'tuple[Any, tuple[str, ...]] | None':
    Parse JSON that strict parsing refused only because of repeated keys; last value wins.

def parse_content(content: 'str', refusal_phrases: 'tuple[str, ...]') -> 'tuple[Any, str, str | None, bool]':
    Return (parsed, response_verdict, detail, recovered_from_prose).

    The refusal heuristic decides the verdict only when no object can be recovered; whether a
    phrase occurred at all is a separate fact, :func:`refusal_phrase_in`, recorded beside a
    recovered object (H36: a refusal followed by a form is a refusal and a form).

def refusal_phrase_in(content: 'str', refusal_phrases: 'tuple[str, ...]') -> 'str | None':
    The first refusal phrase the text contains, typographic quotes read as straight ones, or None.

def _plain_quotes(text: 'str') -> 'str':
    Fold typographic apostrophes and quotation marks to their ASCII forms.

    The refusal phrase list is written with straight apostrophes; a model that writes
    "I’m sorry" is refusing all the same, and the first live refusal in these
    records was missed for exactly that reason (H22).

def _normalise_whitespace(value: 'str') -> 'str':
    (no docstring)

def _span_occurs(span: 'str', document: 'str', relaxations: 'tuple[str, ...]') -> 'str | None':
    Return None when the span is not in the document, else how it was matched ("verbatim" or a relaxation).

def _grounding_kernel(text: 'str') -> 'str':
    The grounding verdict as the harness gives it, with no relaxation and the value-in-span check on.

    The three lines are the harness's own (``_grounding_verdict`` in ``oracle.py``), read here
    over one JSON object rather than over a variant and its output.


